{"crc":"4cee75a2","kind":"Candidate","payload":{"response":"Looking at the issue, the problem is that `str()` on the `pytest.raises` context variable doesn't behave the same as a normal exception catch. When you do `str(e)` on a caught exception, you get the full error message, but with `pytest.raises`, `str(e)` gives a formatted message including the location and exception type.\n\nThe issue is in the `ExceptionInfo` class. Let me find where `__str__` is defined for the `ExceptionInfo` class.\n\nLooking at the code, I can see that `ExceptionInfo` is likely in `_pytest/_code/code.py` or similar. The issue mentions that `str(e.value)` works correctly, but `str(e)` doesn't. The `__str__` method of `ExceptionInfo` should delegate to `str(self.value)` to match the behavior of a normal exception catch.\n\nLet me search for the `ExceptionInfo` class definition:\n\nThe `ExceptionInfo` class is likely in `_pytest/_code/` directory. Let me check the files mentioned. The issue references `_pytest._code.ExceptionInfo` in the test file. Let me look for the `__str__` method in the `ExceptionInfo` class.\n\nBased on the test in `testing/python/raises.py`:\n```python\ndef test_raises_as_contextmanager(self, testdir):\n    ...\n    def test_simple():\n        with pytest.raises(ZeroDivisionError) as excinfo:\n            assert isinstance(excinfo, _pytest._code.ExceptionInfo)\n            1/0\n        print(excinfo)\n```\n\nThe `ExceptionInfo` class needs to have its `__str__` method return `str(self.value)` instead of the current behavior which includes location info.\n\nLet me find the `ExceptionInfo` class:# path: src/_pytest/_code/code.py\n<<<<<<< SEARCH\n    def __str__(self):\n        if self._excinfo is None:\n            return repr(self)\n        entry = self.traceback[-1]\n        loc = ReprFileLocation(entry.path, entry.lineno + 1, self.exconly())\n        return str(loc)\n=======\n    def __str__(self):\n        if self._excinfo is None:\n            return repr(self)\n        return str(self.value)\n>>>>>>> REPLACE","round":1,"slot":0},"producer":{"instance":"01KZQVF18VP1A5C8NEC1TE2AWD","kind":"drafter","parent":"01KZQVF18PBR2VV7HB3ZS75QEP"},"schema":"Candidate@1","seq":14,"t":1786433213.5639522}
{"crc":"05fe8574","kind":"substrate.TriggerFired","payload":{"factory":"validator","firing_key":null,"input_sha256":"sha256:8c2779bc25eabe2566b9ce6934d72914f6c86d4660a2f180e0d9333fb898ef57","instance":"01KZQVF53W8AXRW6Y7MDD4B4DD","resolved_input":{"response":"Looking at the issue, the problem is that `str()` on the `pytest.raises` context variable doesn't behave the same as a normal exception catch. When you do `str(e)` on a caught exception, you get the full error message, but with `pytest.raises`, `str(e)` gives a formatted message including the location and exception type.\n\nThe issue is in the `ExceptionInfo` class. Let me find where `__str__` is defined for the `ExceptionInfo` class.\n\nLooking at the code, I can see that `ExceptionInfo` is likely in `_pytest/_code/code.py` or similar. The issue mentions that `str(e.value)` works correctly, but `str(e)` doesn't. The `__str__` method of `ExceptionInfo` should delegate to `str(self.value)` to match the behavior of a normal exception catch.\n\nLet me search for the `ExceptionInfo` class definition:\n\nThe `ExceptionInfo` class is likely in `_pytest/_code/` directory. Let me check the files mentioned. The issue references `_pytest._code.ExceptionInfo` in the test file. Let me look for the `__str__` method in the `ExceptionInfo` class.\n\nBased on the test in `testing/python/raises.py`:\n```python\ndef test_raises_as_contextmanager(self, testdir):\n    ...\n    def test_simple():\n        with pytest.raises(ZeroDivisionError) as excinfo:\n            assert isinstance(excinfo, _pytest._code.ExceptionInfo)\n            1/0\n        print(excinfo)\n```\n\nThe `ExceptionInfo` class needs to have its `__str__` method return `str(self.value)` instead of the current behavior which includes location info.\n\nLet me find the `ExceptionInfo` class:# path: src/_pytest/_code/code.py\n<<<<<<< SEARCH\n    def __str__(self):\n        if self._excinfo is None:\n            return repr(self)\n        entry = self.traceback[-1]\n        loc = ReprFileLocation(entry.path, entry.lineno + 1, self.exconly())\n        return str(loc)\n=======\n    def __str__(self):\n        if self._excinfo is None:\n            return repr(self)\n        return str(self.value)\n>>>>>>> REPLACE","round":1,"slot":0},"trigger_id":"validate"},"producer":null,"schema":"substrate.TriggerFired@1","seq":15,"t":1786433213.5640872}
